      "passage": "One balmy day at the start of June, Gary was given an important responsibility—caring for his neighbours' cat, Eva, for a month. She was a grey tabby with an inquisitive streak. She enjoyed exploring the neighbourhood by herself and had met most residents. Gary's neighbours, Uncle Tim and Auntie Daisy, were going to another part of the country to check on their new house. Gary was eager to prove himself and took his role seriously. Each morning, he would go into his neighbours' backyard to replenish her food and water. He would even play with Eva. She would come and go as she pleased using the pet door installed on the back door. After a week, Eva began appearing on Gary's porch every morning instead, stretching lazily in the sun, waiting for Gary. Gary thought, \"Great! I don't have to go to the backyard anymore since Eva comes to my porch every day.\" Just when everything felt perfect, disaster struck.\n\nUnder the evening sky, Gary was watering the plants in his garden. Suddenly, a sharp yowl pierced the air. Gary spun around and saw the tail lights of a motorcycle which was speeding down the deserted road. Near the kerb, Eva lay motionless, her fur ruffled, one paw curled awkwardly beneath her face. Panic surged through him. She was still breathing but weak, her body trembling. \"Oh no! What should I do? Would I worsen her injury if I move her?\" He hesitated for a moment before scooping her up in a towel, cradling her like fragile glass. His mother rushed to his side. \"We'll take her to the vet now,\" she said firmly, already holding the car keys.\n\nThe drive to the vet felt like hours. Gary's mother placed a reassuring hand on his shoulder. \"She'll be okay,\" she said. Inside the clinic, the sterile scent of antiseptic filled the air, and then came the hardest part—waiting. How badly was Eva hurt? What would the vet say? Would she be able to recover? Time crawled by as the vet examined Eva thoroughly and administered the treatment. Finally, after an hour, the vet told them Eva's condition—a fractured paw, bruised body but no other broken bones. Her paw was bandaged. She would need to stay indoors for weeks to heal. On the way home, Gary's mother bought a cage.\n\nGary dedicated himself to her recovery. He placed the cage in his room and padded it with towels. Then, he placed Eva carefully into the cage. \"Be good, Eva. You mustn't move too much so that your paw will recover faster,\" Gary said as he patted Eva and closed the cage door. Strangely, Eva did not protest. \"Looks like Eva understands what you said,\" Gary's mother chuckled. For the next few days, Gary changed Eva's bandage daily and made sure Eva had a comfortable cage rest. Once her paw healed and the swelling subsided, Gary let Eva out of the cage. He monitored Eva's movement in his room. Whenever Gary sat on the floor, Eva would curl up beside him and purr softly. She even rubbed her face against his arm or leg. Gary wondered why Eva did that. He checked online and discovered that Eva was showing him affection. \"Mum! Eva loves me!\" Gary ran to his mother and showed her what he had read. \"Of course, she does. Don't you love her too? You've taken such good care of her,\" Gary's mother answered, giving Gary a pat on his back. \"I'm proud of you!\" Gary grinned broadly.\n\nUncle Tim and Auntie Daisy came back and thanked Gary and his mother. Uncle Tim announced, \"We'll be moving to our new house in two weeks' time. We're already in our seventies and the smaller new house is easier to maintain. Now we have to drive five hours to visit our daughter. The new house is within walking distance from my daughter's home. We'll miss you. You're such good neighbours all these years.\" Tears welled up in Gary's eyes when he heard that and saw Auntie Daisy picking up Eva. He stifled his sobs and ran to his room. Gary's mother explained to her neighbours how Gary's relationship with Eva had changed over the month. Meanwhile, Eva was meowing loudly in Auntie Daisy's arms.\n\nOn the night before Uncle Tim and Auntie Daisy moved to their new home, Gary's doorbell rang. Gary opened the door and received a surprise. Uncle Tim handed Gary a box with Eva inside. \"Gary, we would like you to take care of Eva from now on. We know that she will be in good hands,\" Uncle Tim said. \"Don't you think one good turn deserves another?\" Auntie Daisy added with a wink. Gary was at a loss for words as he clutched the box tightly. Gary's mother thanked Uncle Tim. As they turned to leave, Gary put down the box, ran to the elderly couple and gave them a bear hug. \"I promise to take VERY good care of Eva,\" Gary said with heartfelt gratitude.",
